<p class="short">PERSEID METEOR SHOWER</p><p class="short">PERSEID METEOR SHOWER</p>
<p><img alt="" data-nw="1080" decoding="async" height="530" loading="lazy" src="/images/perseids/PPattichis4.jpg" width="800"/></p>
<p class="short"><strong><a href="https://www.ppattichisphotography.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Image Credits: Philippos Pattichis ©</a></strong></p>
<p class="short"><strong>PERSEID METEOR SHOWER 2020</strong></p>
<p class="short"> Perseids are probably the most popular meteor shower of the year.</p>
<p>They occur every year between July 17 and August 24.</p>
<p>Most people are on their Summer holidays and have the chance to sit back on their chairs, relax and watch the Perseids sky show.</p>
<p>The last-quarter moon will appear during the shower this year, and the bright light of the Moon will make observations difficult.</p>
<p> Spectators can expect to see the greatest number of meteors during the shower's peak on the morning of August 12, according to NASA.</p>
<p>In Cyprus the best time to observe the Perseids meteor shower is the night of August 12 and morning of August 13.</p>
<p>In years without moonlight, observers are able to observe higher rates of meteors per hour.</p>
<p>In outburst years the rate can be between 150-200 meteors an hour.</p>
<div class="blocks"><div class="row split"><div class="block media"><p><img alt="" data-nw="1080" decoding="async" height="449" loading="lazy" src="/images/perseids/PPattichis6.jpg" width="600"/></p></div><div class="block"><p><strong><a href="https://www.ppattichisphotography.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Image Credits: Philippos Pattichis ©</a></strong></p><p><strong>To best see the Perseids, go to the darkest possible location and lean back to observe as much sky as possible directly above you.</strong></p><p><strong>The best time to look for meteors is in the pre-dawn hours.</strong></p><p><strong>You should be able to observe a spectacular meteor shower show any night between August 11 and August 13, </strong> <strong>but midnight to dawn any morning the week before or after should produce a few meteors. </strong> </p></div></div></div>





<div class="blocks"><div class="row split reverse"><div class="block media"><p><img alt="" data-nw="1080" decoding="async" height="514" loading="lazy" src="/images/perseids/PPattichis7.jpg" width="600"/></p></div><div class="block"><p class="short"><strong><a href="https://www.ppattichisphotography.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Image Credits: Philippos Pattichis ©</a></strong></p><p>Meteors or "shooting stars" as many people call them, come from comets.</p><p>Most of the annual meteor showers we observe take place as Earth passes through trails of debris left behind  by active comets orbiting the Sun, casting off little bits of dusty debris in their long tails.</p><p>The Perseid meteors come from a comet called Swift-Tuttle, which orbits the Sun every 133 years.</p><p>They took their name from the constellation of Perseus, as they appear to radiate from a point high in the north, called the "radiant in the constellation of Perseus.</p><p>To observe the shower, you need only point yourself generally toward the north and look up.</p></div></div></div>








